    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=906090": {
      "title": "Associate Professor",
      "description": "Associate Professor Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 19/9/2026 Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Global campuses in Singapore, Malaysia, Dubai, Mauritius & Colombo Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation & collaboration Job Reference Number: 1675320 About the Role: We are seeking an internationally recognised scholar and educator in Space and Planetary Science to join the Space Science and Technology Centre (SSTC) within the School of Earth and Planetary Sciences (EPS) as an Associate Professor. This is an outstanding opportunity for an established leader in planetary science to shape research, teaching and engagement activities within one of the world's leading planetary science groups. The successful candidate will bring an internationally prominent research profile, significant experience working across multiple research institutions and international collaborations, and a demonstrated ability to translate scientific discovery into global impact. The position will play a pivotal role in advancing Curtin's planetary science research agenda through leadership of major research initiatives, participation in international space missions, development of strategic partnerships, attraction of competitive funding, and mentorship of early and mid-career researchers. The successful candidate will also provide academic leadership within the School through the development and delivery of innovative teaching programs in Space and Planetary Sciences, supervision of Higher Degree by Research students, and contribution to the continued growth of Curtin's new Bachelor of Science major in Space and Planetary Sciences. Your responsibilities will include: Provide academic and research leadership within the Space Science and Technology Centre and the broader School of EPS. Lead internationally recognised research programs in planetary science, astrobiology, remote sensing, meteorite science, cometary science, planetary geology or related disciplines. Secure and lead nationally and internationally competitive research grants and collaborative research initiatives. Build and maintain strategic research partnerships with universities, research organisations, government agencies, industry and international space organisations. Contribute to international space missions and large-scale collaborative scientific programs. Lead and mentor research staff and Higher Degree by Research candidates, fostering the development of the next generation of planetary scientists. Develop and deliver innovative undergraduate and postgraduate teaching programs in planetary science, astrobiology and remote sensing of planetary surfaces. Lead curriculum innovation, including the development of new units and courses aligned with emerging priorities in planetary exploration and space science. Promote Curtin and the SSTC through public engagement, media activities, keynote presentations and professional service roles nationally and internationally. Contribute to School, Faculty and University leadership activities as required. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): Essential Qualifications PhD in Planetary Science, Space Science, Astrobiology, Planetary Geology, Remote Sensing or a closely related discipline. International Research Standing An internationally recognised research profile in planetary science demonstrated through a substantial publication record, including publications in leading journals such as the Nature and/or Science family of journals. Significant postdoctoral and academic experience gained across multiple internationally recognised research institutions or laboratories. Research Leadership and Funding Success A sustained and successful track record of securing nationally and internationally competitive research funding and leading externally funded research programs. Evidence of successful leadership of research groups, centres, institutes or major collaborative research programs. Demonstrated excellence in Higher Degree by Research supervision, including completions and mentorship of early and mid-career researchers. Planetary Science Expertise Demonstrated expertise in planetary science research, including experience in one or more of meteorite science, cometary science, planetary surface processes, astrobiology or planetary remote sensing. Significant field research experience, including undertaking and leading scientific field campaigns in remote or challenging environments. Demonstrated involvement in international space missions or major planetary science programs, including experience as a Science Team member or equivalent scientific leadership role. Teaching and Curriculum Leadership A strong record of teaching excellence in planetary science and related disciplines, including experience coordinating units, establishing new courses and developing innovative curricula. Demonstrated capability to teach planetary science, planetary remote sensing and astrobiology at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Academic Leadership and Engagement Outstanding communication, stakeholder engagement and public speaking skills, including the ability to engage effectively with scientific, industry, government and community audiences. Demonstrated commitment to collegiality, diversity, inclusion, integrity and research excellence. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia. We will also consider international candidates who are eligible for a relevant work visa. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check Your new Team – School of Earth and Planetary Sciences: The School incorporates geology, spatial sciences, advanced analytical techniques, geophysics, planetary science, and more, to provide world-class training for planetary, geoscientific and geospatial careers, and undertake fundamental and applied research into the past, present and future of our planet and solar system, recognised by the latest Excellence in Research for Australia assessment. With an impressive suite of facilities and expertise, and a range of funding from academic and industry sources, our School undertakes research projects all over the world in most fields of geoscience and planetary science. A core value of our school is to deliver teaching excellence for undergraduate and Higher Degree by Research students, which is presented in a broad field of units and courses. For more information about the school, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/learning-teaching/science-engineering/school-of-earth-and-planetary-sciences/ Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we are passionate about fostering an inclusive, diverse and welcoming environment in which all people feel respected, valued and safe. Curtin is dedicated to promoting gender equity and inclusivity in all areas. We are proud of our achievements to date and recognise that there is more to do to improve employment outcomes for women and people of diverse gender identities, particularly in STEMM disciplines. As an Athena SWAN accredited institution, we celebrate diversity and actively work to advance the representation of women and gender-diverse people in STEMM areas and empower individuals so they can thrive in their careers through a range of supportive initiatives, including leadership training, mentorship for early career progression and promotion, networking opportunities, and other professional development resources. As reflected in the Athena Swan Charter, gender equality is a broader issue that extends beyond barriers affecting women alone, and we are committed to addressing these challenges holistically. Additionally, we offer staff a competitive remuneration and benefits package, including but not limited to: 17% superannuation, plus a variety of salary packaging options. Paid Parental leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver and 3 weeks for non-primary) subject to eligibility criteria outlined in the Enterprise Agreement; personal leave (14 days) and annual leave (20 days) Enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year (including Easter Tuesday!), and 2 extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year Period (see EBA 51.1 and 51.9). Breastfeeding facilities and campus-based childcare (where priority of placement is given to Curtin staff and students) Flexible work arrangements including return to work provisions On-campus medical centre Academic Carer Support Scheme and other diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives detailed via the below link. We believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds to join us in shaping the future of STEMM together and making tomorrow better. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This continuing ALD level role attracts a remuneration of $178,845 - $196,832 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a variety food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience A document (up to 1500 words) addressing the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Wednesday, 14 October 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. #LI-DNI Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=908240": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer - Social Work",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer - Social Work Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 24/9/2026 Full time, permanent | Curtin Bentley Campus Be part of an exciting and dynamic social work team Great benefits and work environment Reference Number: 1676199 About the Role: The Curtin School of Allied Health is seeking to appoint a permanent Teaching and Research (T&R) Academic, Senior Lecturer in Social Work. This role is central to the Curtin School of Allied Health’s commitment to excellence in scholarly teaching practices and research standing across the Bachelor of Social Work and Master of Social Work (Qualifying). We are seeking a critically reflective and student-centred social work educator and researcher who can design and deliver high-quality teaching across a range of content areas and lead and contribute to research and higher degree supervision. Collegiality is important at both the social work program level and the broader School level, including through active contribution to program development and interprofessional practice across the Curtin School of Allied Health (CSAH). The successful applicant will bring relevant social work expertise and a commitment to the continued growth, quality and impact of our social work curriculum and program research. Your responsibilities will include: Teaching Design and deliver inclusive, engaging learning activities across various formats (online, face-to-face) to enhance student learning outcomes and readiness for practice. Collaborate with colleagues to innovate curriculum and develop effective learning materials, while assessing student needs and providing necessary support. Maintaining awareness of current discipline practices and contributing to professional development in higher education. Assess and provide feedback on learning to students. Manage administrative tasks related to course delivery, including assessment moderation, and ensure that curricular innovations accommodate diverse student needs for a successful university experience. Participate as an active member of the social work program and CSAH in meetings, working groups and engagement activities as required. Research Play a significant role in research projects, including, where appropriate, leadership of a research team/project. Actively seek and act as co or chief investigator in applications for competitive research grants and/or fellowships. Disseminate research findings through seminars, workshops and conferences. Publish/exhibit in high-quality refereed journals/outlets in manner consistent with disciplinary practice. Undertake principal supervision of research honours, research masters, and research doctoral students. Provide research support and mentoring of social work colleagues as appropriate. Service and Leadership Provide leadership in relation to school/faculty–wide initiatives Contribute to academic engagement and collegiality, in fostering a supportive working environment. Participate in community engagement and external activities relevant to social work. Demonstrate professional behaviours consistent with the AASW Code of Ethics and the University Code of Conduct and Vision, Mission and Values. If you are passionate about social work, we encourage you to apply and become a part of our dedicated team at Curtin University. Your expertise and commitment will help us shape the future of social work education. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Eligibility for membership of the Australian Association of Social Workers (AASW) A doctoral qualification in social work or a discipline relevant to social work. Demonstrated knowledge and experience of pedagogies appropriate to and in keeping with contemporary educational practice relevant to social work. Demonstrated experience in curriculum development and unit/course coordination. Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal and organisational skills Demonstrated commitment to the development of a collegial and supportive working environment and the ability to interact with students and staff with sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to the application of relevant and applicable policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the functions of this position. An established record of research outputs in refereed academic journals/outlets consistent with social work. Record of obtaining research income, including competitive research grants and/or fellowships. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new team: School of Allied Health The School of Allied Health at Curtin University is a vibrant, collaborative, and community-focused school dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of people across Western Australia and beyond. Our teaching, research, and partnerships span a wide range of allied health disciplines, including exercise and sport science, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, speech pathology and social work. We are deeply committed to delivering an exceptional student experience - one that prepares graduates to be compassionate, skilled, and practice-ready professionals who make meaningful contributions to the communities they serve. Our staff are passionate educators, researchers, and practitioners who work closely with industry partners, health services, and community organisations to ensure our programs remain relevant, innovative, and responsive to emerging health needs. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time permanent, teaching & research academic position, with a salary range between $148,847 - $171,344 (ALC) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Thursday, 22 October 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=908433": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 24/9/2026 Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Based in Kalgoorlie in the heart of WA's most significant mining region Join a high-impact geotechnical research project Job Reference Number: 1680589 Curtin University is seeking a motivated Research Associate to join a collaborative industry-focused research project investigating the long-term weathering and strength degradation of sulphide-bearing waste rock and its implications for open-pit slope stability at the Wodgina lithium operation. About the Role: Working in collaboration with Mineral Resources Limited (MinRes), the project is supported by Curtin’s Resources Technology and Critical Minerals Trailblazer, which brings together industry and university research to accelerate innovation and research translation in the resources and critical minerals sector. Working closely with Curtin researchers and MinRes, you will undertake an integrated program of laboratory experimentation, advanced material characterisation, and numerical modelling to better understand how waste rock properties evolve over time and influence long-term slope performance. This role presents an exciting opportunity to contribute to applied geotechnical research with direct impact on mining operations while advancing innovative approaches to rock mass degradation assessment and modelling. Reporting to the Project Lead, your responsibilities will include: Designing and conducting laboratory experiments to investigate time-dependent weathering and degradation behaviour of sulphide-bearing, foliated meta-sedimentary waste rock. Planning and undertaking sample selection, preparation, conditioning and site-relevant geochemical exposure testing. Coordinating and interpreting mechanical testing programs to assess changes in stiffness, strength and anisotropic behaviour over time. Working with the John de Laeter Centre to integrate mineralogical and microstructural characterisation techniques, including XRD, SEM-EDS, petrography and micro-CT analysis. Developing, calibrating and validating discrete element modelling (DEM) approaches to simulate degradation processes and slope behaviour. Translating laboratory and modelling outcomes into practical recommendations, including degradation factors, domain susceptibility rankings, monitoring triggers and mitigation strategies. Contributing to project reporting, industry engagement and publication of research outcomes. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): A PhD (or thesis submitted and near conferral) in Geotechnical Engineering or Mining Engineering. Demonstrated experience in laboratory testing of geomaterials, rock mechanics or geotechnical characterisation. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills with an interest in experimental and numerical research. Demonstrated experience with numerical modelling tools, particularly DEM-based approaches, will be highly regarded. The ability to interpret and integrate complex geological, mechanical and geochemical datasets. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Strong written and verbal communication skills and the ability to work effectively with academic and industry stakeholders and contribute within a multidisciplinary research team. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new Team – The Western Australian School of Mines (WASM): WASM, part of Curtin University's Faculty of Science and Engineering, is a globally recognised leader in mining, metallurgy, energy and chemical engineering. Operating from campuses in Perth and Kalgoorlie, WASM combines world-class teaching, research and industry collaboration to address some of the resources sector's most complex challenges, with a strong focus on sustainability, innovation and real-world impact. For more than 120 years, WASM has helped shape the global resources industry through research excellence, industry partnerships and the development of future leaders. Ranked among the world's leading institutions for Minerals and Mining Engineering, the School continues to drive innovation in areas including geomechanics, automation, digital technologies, energy transition and sustainable resource development. To find out more about the school, please visit: https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/learning-teaching/science-engineering/wa-school-of-mines/. Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we value the diversity of the community and are passionate about fostering an inclusive culture in which all people feel respected, valued, welcome and safe. The Faculty of Science and Engineering is committed to equity and opportunity in all aspects of employment including recruitment, career development, promotion and workplace support. To demonstrate this commitment, the faculty has developed a Diversity Awareness Program with a key focus on the areas of gender equity, diverse sex, sexuality and gender, ethnicity, disability and accessibility, and health and wellness. We aim to promote and improve awareness and participation of diversity and inclusion initiatives through the appointment of diversity representatives and male champions of change across the faculty. As a university and faculty, we believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage applications from every background, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, women, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and racially diverse backgrounds to join the Faculty of Science and Engineering and make tomorrow better. Applicants with disability who require adjustments, or alternative methods of communication in the recruitment process can contact our friendly team at curtincareers@curtin.edu.au. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/. For more information on Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Plan, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/curtin-2030-strategic-plan/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This 12-month fixed term full-time ALA level role attracts a remuneration of $108,064 - $115,862 (ALA.6 – ALA.8) + 17% superannuation, and locality allowance. You may also receive an extra 15 hours (or 2 days) of annual leave each year if you are required to travel to Perth. Situated at Curtin’s historic Kalgoorlie (Karlkurla) campus, our iconic school is located on the doorstep of the flourishing mining industry. The campus offers strong opportunities for staff and students to collaborate with the resources industry in the Goldfields region of Western Australia, in not only traditional metalliferous mining but in the burgeoning battery minerals sector. To learn more about Kalgoorlie please visit https://study.curtin.edu.au/curtin-life/regional-study/curtin-kalgoorlie/ For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience A document addressing the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Thursday, 15 October 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=908469": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Social Work",
      "description": "Lecturer - Social Work Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Part Time 24/9/2026 Part time (0.6 FTE), permanent | Curtin Bentley Campus Be part of an exciting and dynamic social work team Great benefits and work environment Reference Number: 1677374 About the Role: The Curtin School of Allied Health is seeking to appoint a permanent Teaching Academic Lecturer in Social Work, at level B (0.6FTE). This role is central to the Curtin School of Allied Health’s commitment to excellence in teaching and learning, and to providing a rigorous, inclusive and engaging student experience across the Bachelor of Social Work and Master of Social Work (Qualifying). We are seeking critically reflective and student-centred social work practitioners and/or educators who can design and deliver high-quality teaching across a range of content areas, coordinate units effectively, support diverse learners, and contribute to curriculum quality, innovation and the scholarship of learning and teaching. Collegiality is important at both the social work program level and the broader School level, including through active contribution to program development and interprofessional practice across the Curtin School of Allied Health (CSAH). The successful applicant will bring relevant social work expertise, contemporary practice knowledge, and a commitment to contributing to the continued growth, quality and impact of our social work programs. Your responsibilities will include: Design and deliver inclusive, engaging learning activities across various formats (online, face-to-face) to enhance student learning outcomes and readiness for practice. Collaborate with colleagues to innovate curriculum and develop effective learning materials, while assessing student needs and providing necessary support. Maintaining awareness of current discipline practices and contributing to professional development in higher education. Assess and provide feedback on learning to students. Manage administrative tasks related to course delivery, including assessment moderation, and ensure that curricular innovations accommodate diverse student needs for a successful university experience. Participate as an active member of the social work program and CSAH in meetings, working groups and engagement activities as required. If you are passionate about social work, we encourage you to apply and become a part of our dedicated team at Curtin University. Your expertise and commitment will help us shape the future of social work education. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Eligibility for membership of the Australian Association of Social Workers (AASW) Masters degree or PhD. We welcome applicants who may not hold a Masters or PhD qualification; please outline your equivalent experience in your cover letter with reference to Level 9 in the following document: https://tinyurl.com/3r6yfaz4 (please copy and paste link into your search browser). An understanding of or willingness to develop knowledge of pedagogy and contemporary educational practice relevant to social work. Demonstrated capacity / experience to coordinate activities and/or projects with the ability to apply these to unit coordination. Demonstrated high level communication and interpersonal and organisational skills Demonstrated commitment to the development of a collegial and supportive working environment and the ability to interact with students and staff with sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to the application of relevant and applicable policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day performance of the functions of this position. Understanding of and demonstrated commitment to principles of social justice, critical perspectives in social work and social work ethics. Demonstrated capacity to facilitate learning using a reflective approach. Desirable An emerging record of contribution to scholarship in teaching. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new team: School of Allied Health The School of Allied Health at Curtin University is a vibrant, collaborative, and community-focused school dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of people across Western Australia and beyond. Our teaching, research, and partnerships span a wide range of allied health disciplines, including exercise and sport science, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, speech pathology and social work. We are deeply committed to delivering an exceptional student experience - one that prepares graduates to be compassionate, skilled, and practice-ready professionals who make meaningful contributions to the communities they serve. Our staff are passionate educators, researchers, and practitioners who work closely with industry partners, health services, and community organisations to ensure our programs remain relevant, innovative, and responsive to emerging health needs. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a part time (0.6 FTE) permanent, teaching academic position, with a salary range between $ 121,857 - $144,352 (ALB) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Thursday, 22 October 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=908664": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Senior Research Fellow Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 27/9/2026 4-year fixed term, full-time appointment (possible extension) Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Join an inclusive team that fosters innovation, collaboration & sustainability Job Reference Number: 1687850 Shape the future of AI for nature and society How can artificial intelligence help protect Australia's biodiversity while ensuring decisions are ethical, inclusive and socially responsible? Curtin University is seeking an exceptional Senior Research Fellow (Level C) to join a groundbreaking research project investigating how AI-enabled biodiversity assessment and monitoring can embrace justice practices to achieve Australia's conservation goals. The project aims to develop new frameworks and practical guidance for the responsible use of artificial intelligence in biodiversity monitoring, reporting and conservation decision-making. About the Role: This is a unique opportunity to contribute to nationally significant research with global relevance, helping position Australia as a leader in the development of justice-oriented technologies for environmental stewardship. As Senior Research Fellow, you will provide intellectual leadership across the project, driving high-quality research and strengthening engagement with academic, government, industry and community stakeholders. Your responsibilities will include: Leading and contributing to internationally recognised research outputs. Developing innovative, high-impact research programs and funding opportunities. Building interdisciplinary collaborations across environmental, social science and technology-focused disciplines. Undertaking stakeholder engagement and co-designing activities with project partners. Mentoring and supervising higher degree by research students and early career researchers. Translating research findings into policy-relevant outputs, guidance materials and practical frameworks. Contributing to Curtin's growing reputation for world-class research addressing complex societal challenges. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): A PhD in a relevant discipline such as environmental governance, sustainability, conservation science, public policy, science and technology studies, sociology, geography, information systems or a related field. A strong track record of high-quality research publications and demonstrated research impact. Experience securing, leading or contributing to externally funded research projects. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work effectively across disciplines and sectors. Demonstrated experience supervising research students and contributing to research leadership. Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the following areas: Environmental governance or conservation policy Artificial intelligence and society Responsible innovation and technology governance Digital technologies for environmental monitoring Participatory, co-design or community-engaged research methods Ethics, justice and equity in decision-making systems Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new Team – School of Molecular and Life Sciences (MLS): The School of MLS encompasses 165 academic, research and professional staff, supporting learning for over 1000 students. The school comprises the disciplines of chemistry, agriculture and food, and ecology. We are an ambitious, multi-disciplinary and industry-focused team that seek to equip our students and graduates with the skills they need to make tomorrow better. Academics from different disciplines work together to perform high-quality teaching and support each other in achieving at the highest possible levels in all aspects of academic life. For more information, please visit: https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/learning-teaching/science-engineering/school-of-molecular-life-sciences/ Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we are passionate about fostering an inclusive, diverse and welcoming environment in which all people feel respected, valued and safe. Curtin is dedicated to promoting gender equity and inclusivity in all areas. We are proud of our achievements to date and recognise that there is more to do to improve employment outcomes for women and people of diverse gender identities, particularly in STEMM disciplines. As an Athena SWAN accredited institution, we celebrate diversity and actively work to advance the representation of women and gender-diverse people in STEMM areas and empower individuals so they can thrive in their careers through a range of supportive initiatives, including leadership training, mentorship for early career progression and promotion, networking opportunities, and other professional development resources. As reflected in the Athena Swan Charter, gender equality is a broader issue that extends beyond barriers affecting women alone, and we are committed to addressing these challenges holistically. Additionally, we offer staff a competitive remuneration and benefits package, including but not limited to: 17% superannuation, plus a variety of salary packaging options. Paid Parental leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver and 3 weeks for non-primary) subject to eligibility criteria outlined in the Enterprise Agreement; personal leave (14 days) and annual leave (20 days) Enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year (including Easter Tuesday!), and 2 extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year Period (see EBA 51.1 and 51.9). Breastfeeding facilities and campus-based childcare (where priority of placement is given to Curtin staff and students) Flexible work arrangements including return to work provisions On-campus medical centre Academic Carer Support Scheme and other diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives detailed via the below link. We believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds to join us in shaping the future of STEMM together and making tomorrow better. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This 4-year fixed term, full-time ALC level role attracts a remuneration of $148,847 - $171,344 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a variety food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience A document addressing the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Tuesday, 27 October 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. #LI-DNI Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=881217": {
      "title": "Casual Academic EOI - Mechatronic Engineering",
      "description": "Casual Academic EOI - Mechatronic Engineering Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Casual 13/7/2026 Flexible casual teaching across lectures, tutorials, workshops & lab sessions Share your expertise & help shape the next generation of mechatronic engineers Join the talent register & be considered for future sessional teaching roles The School of Civil and Mechanical Engineering (CME) welcomes expressions of interest from suitably qualified academics and industry professionals who would like to be considered for casual academic (sessional) teaching opportunities within the Mechatronic Engineering discipline. While there are currently no existing vacancies, we are establishing a register of potential sessional staff to support future teaching needs as they arise. Applicants with relevant academic qualifications, industry experience, and a demonstrated commitment to high‑quality learning and teaching are encouraged to submit an expression of interest (refer to selection criteria below). Successful candidates may be contacted when short‑term, casual, or sessional teaching opportunities become available across our programs. Please note - submitting an EOI does not guarantee employment, but it ensures you will be considered for upcoming opportunities that align with your expertise. Accountabilities and responsibilities may include: Contributing to the teaching activities of the school by delivering: Lectures Tutorials Practical classes Demonstrations Workshops Student field excursions Computer laboratory sessions Prepare and deliver lectures, seminars, and other learning activities as required. Mark assessments and undertake associated tasks related to the evaluation of student work. Work collaboratively as part of a teaching team, typically under the direction of a unit coordinator. Professional Knowledge and Conduct: Maintain up to date knowledge about legislation, standards, practices, policies and procedures pertinent to teaching area. Maintain confidentiality of student grades at all times. Consult with students in a professional and equitable manner and comply with University protocols, policies and procedures and relevant appropriate legislation and governing body requirements. Display openness to diversity of opinion and adapts behaviour to be helpful and considerate. Perform a range of administrative functions connected to the subject in which the teaching is conducted Behave consistently with the Code of Conduct and Curtin’s Values. Role Requirements: You will require work rights in Australia that match the tenure of the appointment, and living in WA as face-to-face teaching will be required. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Selection Criteria: Essential: Tertiary qualification(s) in a relevant discipline. Demonstrated ability to contribute to a range of learning opportunities for students that are relevant to the learning outcomes. Demonstrated excellent oral and written communication skills Demonstrated organisational skills including the ability to meet deadlines. Demonstrated ability to learn and use software systems/technology to advance student success and retention. Demonstrated ability to maintain quality standards in teaching and assessment. Desirable Criteria: Completion of a higher degree by research or recognised equivalent qualification. Experience in teaching and learning. Professional experience in relevant field. Commitment to inter-professional education (IPE), innovative and collegial principles in the provision of education services. Experience implementing effective practices supporting student transition, success, and retention. About the School of CME: The School of CME delivers undergraduate and postgraduate courses, and high-quality research in civil and mechanical engineering. Curtin is recognised internationally as a leader in graduate research studies and for the breadth and quality of its research higher degree programs and offers Master of Philosophy and Doctor of Philosophy degrees in Civil Engineering and Mechanical Engineering. The School has a student retention rate 90% retained in School programs, with 93% retained at Curtin, one of the highest student-satisfaction rates across the university. The School of CME is a collegial working environment, which undertakes outstanding theoretical and applied research, and runs highly regarded and popular teaching programs at the undergraduate and postgraduate levels. We firmly believe that our people are our most valuable asset, so we work to grow and diversify the skills, knowledge, and capability of all our staff. We embrace flexibility to allow our people to manage the changing demands of work, personal and family life. Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we value the diversity of the community and are passionate about fostering an inclusive culture in which all people feel respected, valued, welcome and safe. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/. About Curtin Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Submitting an EOI: If this sounds like an opportunity that aligns with your next career steps, we encourage you to select APPLY NOW , ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages. Please note applicants are not required to address each element of the selection criteria, but should provide sufficient information in their application to enable the selection panel to make an informed assessment of their suitability for the role. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address. There is currently no closing date for the expression of interest process. Successful candidates will only be contacted where qualifications and expertise meet teaching availability, and demand for casual academic work exists. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert at any time. #LI-DNI Back to search results"
